  

<h2 id="how-another-go-at-keeping-a-plain-text-calendar-is-helping-me-clarify-what-goes-where">How another go at keeping a plain text calendar is helping me clarify what goes where</h2>
<p>Making and using calendars is viscerally satisfying to me. I love the sense of stability and preparedness I get from seeing dates laid out in neat rows!</p>
<p>Along with that comes the irresistible idea of writing notes to my future self. This could mean recording tasks that need to be done on or by on specific dates, but it’s much more than that. In as real a sense as I can muster, my calendar is an attempt to communicate ideas that I believe future me will need to see. The simpler and more hands-on, the better.</p>
<h3 id="why-hello-again-plain-text-calendar">Why, hello again, plain text calendar!</h3>
<p>That’s why <a href="https://sappelen.com/en/2026/10/06/your-calendar-in-a-google-sheet/" target="_blank">Marjon’s calendar in a Google Sheet</a> has inspired me to return to the world of plain text calendars. <a href="https://ellanew.com/2026/05/11/plain-text-calendar-todo-txt" class="wikilink">Unlike last time</a>, I’m not trying to replace my digital calendar. Just provide a supplementary, intentional, <em>optional</em> layer that brings my plans into sharper focus.</p>
<p>My latest plain text calendar is a simple outline of the current year in one document, with events indented underneath each date.</p>
<p>Like this:</p>
<pre><code># Quarter 4, 2026  

## October  

### week 42  

- [ ] 10/12 MON  
  - [ ] 1100 Westpac meeting  
- [ ] 10/13 TUE  
  - [ ] 1030 Cleaning job  
  - [ ] 1230 Visit Y  
- 10/14 WED  
- 10/15 THU  
- 10/16 FRI  
- 10/17 SAT  
- 10/18 SUN  
</code></pre>
<p>I turn the next few days into checkboxes, and use search or an Obsidian plugin (like Simple Checkboxes) to show just those days and events in my side bar.</p>
<h3 id="why-not-just-use-regular-apps">Why not just use regular apps?</h3>
<p>Someone somewhere is scratching their head and asking why I don’t just use a regular task manager designed to surface tasks to an agenda according to the date assigned to it. Or why I don’t use Emacs for both calendar and tasks.</p>
<p>The answer is simple: I feel more settled when my tasks live in text files that only need an ordinary search box to work. That means they are completely portable between platforms and devices and do not require software (<span class="small-caps">FOSS</span> or not) to work, or exporting to be in an open format.</p>
<p>I’ve proven to myself that <a href="https://ellanew.com/2026/02/23/filter-todotxt-in-obsidian-no-plugins" class="wikilink">it’s possible to bend an app like Obsidian to surface tasks in much the same way as a dedicated task manager</a>, but right now I prefer to remain as app-independent as I can.</p>
<h3 id="notifications-are-a-safety-net">Notifications are a safety net</h3>
<p>Plain text lists house my tasks, some of which are on project pages. It’s lovely and quiet there, no dates or reminders or push notifications of any kind! Just the way I like it.</p>
<p>Hence, anything that simply <em>must</em> grab my attention on a specific date needs to be in my calendar. If it’s super important and terrible things will happen if I forget, I’ll set a repeating reminder to nag me until I turn it off (which I won’t do, until the thing has been done). This kind of push notification is a safety net, not the trapeze itself.</p>
<p>Lesson: tasks that need doing on or by a specific date are no longer tasks, but events. And as such, they belong on the calendar.</p>
<p>If you feel too busy to copy and paste a task-with-a-deadline from a project page to your calendar, then you are right: you <em>are</em> too busy! It doesn’t take long to do and is a good way to make sure you are on top of what is on your — not too full — plate. I’m not saying everyone should track their tasks and time the way I do, just that the too-busy excuse isn’t a good one.</p>
<h3 id="on-secret-thoughts-and-shared-calendars">On (secret) thoughts and shared calendars</h3>
<p>When I have a shared digital calendar and there’s something I’d like to remember about an event on it that I don’t want the other collaborators to read, I use my personal calendar to create a parallel event.</p>
<p>This puts my secret thoughts where I can see them, while keeping them away from the eyes of the group. Anything super confidential can be put into an encrypted note and linked to from the calendar.</p>
<h3 id="remember-tasks-and-events-do-not-mix">Remember: tasks and events do not mix</h3>
<p>Tasks are like steps in a recipe, while events tell you when to get the dish out of the oven. Keeping individual tasks into your calendar is a sure path to overwhelm.</p>
<p>Each of my tasks belongs to a category, and yours do, too. I don’t need a database-type field to set my categories; a simple number or tag does very well for this. Johnny Decimal’s Life Admin system helped me define my categories.</p>
<p>To add to the food analogy, categories are like menu plans. You want your calendar to tell you when it’s dinner time, not how hot the oven should be.</p>
<p>So while tasks don’t go into your calendar, categories do! You can’t control how long a task will take to complete, but you can budget a set amount of time for each category. You’ll then work on the tasks that belong to it for that duration. I first read about doing this from Carl Pullein, but have since seen the idea in other places, too.</p>
<p>So on Monday morning I have a <em>Writing</em> event in my calendar. When that time comes, I look at my writing related tasks and start working on the one that is next in line.</p>
<p>To summarise:</p>
<ul>
<li>Use whatever calendar you will look at every day<br>
</li>
<li>Only time-related events go into your calendar<br>
</li>
<li>Create events for categories of tasks, and work through that category’s list in order<br>
</li>
<li>Keep private notes about a shared event in a parallel event on your personal calendar, or in an encrypted note linked from it<br>
</li>
<li>A plain text calendar can be an optional focus-promoting layer on top of your usual calendar, rather than a replacement</li>
</ul>
